{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"overview","type":"text","text":"# Worked examples - overview\n\nWorked examples matter because they make modelling decisions visible. They can help learners move from a problem description into a model, compare alternatives, and develop judgement about relationships, abstraction, and granularity.\n\nKeep this canvas clean: use it as a navigation surface. Put detailed evidence logic in the claims and evidence index notes.","x":-920,"y":-280,"width":430,"height":250},
		{"id":"claims","type":"text","text":"# Worked examples - claims\n\n1. Worked examples support independence when they expose reasoning, alternatives, criteria, and revision rather than only a polished final diagram.\n\n2. Examples help calibrate difficult representational decisions such as relation choice, granularity, and cross-family translation.\n\n3. Examples can also create dependence or surface imitation when learners copy visible structures without understanding why they fit.\n\n4. Fading matters: examples should move learners toward justified attempts, not permanent reliance on lecturer solutions.","x":-300,"y":-315,"width":500,"height":320},
		{"id":"evidence_index","type":"text","text":"# Worked examples - evidence index\n\n## Strong support\n\n- L1M2: example-based scaffolding, counterexamples, and rationale-based feedback.\n- L2M3: example-first teaching combined with non-recipe modelling decisions.\n- L3M4: autonomy-first labs with selective examples and decomposition support.\n- S1M5: student request for text-to-model examples and incremental progression.\n- S2M3: relationship modelling learned through completed examples and analogy.\n- S4M3: examples valued when they explain rationale and alternatives.\n- S5M3: examples calibrate detail and support simplification.\n- S6M3: examples connect qualities to design reasoning.\n\n## Qualifications\n\n- L2M2: examples can become a precondition for starting.\n- S3M5: partial examples and slides are insufficient without validation.\n- S6M5: isolated examples do not solve cross-family integration.\n- L4M6: granularity and stopping rules require explicit judgement, not only examples.\n\n## Open checks\n\n- Separate direct student evidence from lecturer interpretation.\n- Treat reconstructed S4-S6 wording cautiously.\n- Check whether examples improved later modelling or mainly felt helpful.","x":395,"y":-430,"width":560,"height":535},
		{"id":"comparison_worked_examples","type":"file","file":"qualitative_analysis/04_cross_case_analysis/comparison_worked_examples.md","x":1170,"y":-320,"width":390,"height":80},
